**What**
`facet-git-tree` is exemplary about backend abstraction - every entry point is
generic over `gix` `Find`/`Write`, and even accepts `?Sized` so a `&dyn Write`
works. `gix-store` throws that away one layer up: `Store` holds a
`&gix::Repository` and every method reaches into `self.repo` directly. The
seam is in the wrong place.

**Where**
`crates/gix-store/src/store.rs:42-46` (`struct Store { repo, data_prefix,
schema_prefix }`), contrasted with `crates/facet-git-tree/src/ser.rs:32-39` and
`src/de.rs:53-58`.

**Why it matters**
Three concrete consequences.

*No test or in-memory backend.* `facet-git-tree` ships `ObjectStore`, an
in-memory odb, and its tests use it. `gix-store` cannot: every test needs a
real repository on a real filesystem (`crates/gix-store/tests/store.rs`,
`test-support`). Anyone who wants to embed the entity model - which is the
genuinely reusable idea here, not the tree encoding - inherits a hard
dependency on a working `gix::Repository`.

*`open_with_prefixes` is half a feature.* The prefixes are stored but never
exposed. `Store` has no `data_prefix()` accessor, so a consumer that opened
with `refs/meta/rules` cannot construct a revision spec against its own
namespace. The CLI proves the point: `resolve_at` at
`crates/git-store/src/main.rs:257-262` builds the spec with a hardcoded literal,
`format!("refs/store/{kind}/{name}{rev}")`, because there is no way to ask the
store what its data prefix is. If `git-store` ever grew a `--namespace` flag it
would silently resolve revisions against the wrong refs. The doc comment on
`open_with_prefixes` says "Refs are this systems public API surface, so the
namespace is configurable" - and then makes the configured value unreadable.

*Error taxonomy collapses at the boundary.* `Error::Git(Box<dyn Error>)`
(`crates/gix-store/src/error.rs:64-66`) swallows every `gix` failure, so a
caller cannot distinguish "object not found" from "ref lock contention" from
"IO error" without string matching. This is the mechanism by which the missing-
schema-after-fetch failure surfaces as an unattributable object-not-found.

**Options**
- Option A - minimal: add `data_prefix()` / `schema_prefix()` accessors. Fixes
  the ref-prefix leak without restructuring anything. The object-not-found
  taxonomy gap is better folded into `0b4a9b27`'s error pass, which already
  adds `Error::SchemaObjectMissing` at the same boundary, rather than solved
  twice with a second, overlapping variant here.
- Option B - parameterize `Store` over a small `Backend` trait covering the
  four things it actually needs (find, write, read ref, compare-and-swap ref),
  with a `gix::Repository` impl and an in-memory impl. Mirrors what
  `facet-git-tree` already does one layer down, and makes the entity model
  independently testable and reusable. Trade-off: ref transactions and locking
  are genuinely harder to abstract than object read/write, and an in-memory
  impl that does not model locking would be a misleading test double.
- Option C - leave it; `gix-store`s README explicitly scopes itself as "the
  untrusted-single-writer-friendly demo primitive". Trade-off: then say so in
  the type, e.g. name it `GixStore`, so nobody mistakes it for the abstraction
  layer.

**Recommendation**
Option A's accessors are worth taking if there is time - small, and they close
a real correctness hole in `open_with_prefixes`. The error-variant half of
Option A is not separate work any more: fold it into `0b4a9b27`'s error pass
instead of adding a second ad-hoc variant here. Neither half is urgent on its
own - this issue's demo impact is `invisible`, so it can slip past the talk
entirely. Option B is the right long-term shape and is worth naming as future
work on a slide, because "why is the bottom layer backend-agnostic and the
middle layer not?" is a fair question from anyone reading the crate list.
Medium confidence on B; the locking argument against it is real.